@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# unsafe-miri-ci
+
+> Run `cargo miri test` in CI for every crate that contains `unsafe` code.
+
+## Why It Matters
+
+Miri interprets Rust tests and detects many classes of undefined behavior in the executions it explores: invalid memory accesses, uninitialized reads, data races, and violations of the selected pointer/aliasing model. It complements code review and tools such as sanitizers, which also dynamically detect classes of memory and concurrency bugs.
+
+Passing Miri is not a proof of soundness. Results depend on executed paths, inputs, schedules, the selected model and supported operations. A test using only safe code can still expose unsoundness in a dependency's safe API.
+
+## Bad
+
+```yaml
+# Ordinary tests alone do not check the unsafe implementation under Miri.
+- name: Test
+  run: cargo test --all-features
+```
+
+## Good
+
+```yaml
+# .github/workflows/miri.yml
+name: Miri
+
+on: [push, pull_request]
+
+jobs:
+  miri:
+    name: Miri (nightly)
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+
+      - name: Install nightly toolchain with Miri
+        run: |
+          rustup toolchain install nightly --component miri
+          rustup override set nightly
+          cargo miri setup
+
+      - name: Run Miri
+        env:
+          MIRIFLAGS: "-Zmiri-strict-provenance"
+        run: cargo miri test --all-features
+```
+
+## Key Points
+
+- **Nightly only**: Miri requires a nightly toolchain. Pin a specific nightly date in `rust-toolchain.toml` if you need reproducible CI.
+- **Cost**: interpretation can be much slower than native tests. Measure the suite's cost and select targeted tests when needed; no universal slowdown factor applies.
+- **`MIRIFLAGS` and models**: check supported flags and the default aliasing model for the pinned nightly. Strict provenance can reject unsupported pointer constructions; Stacked Borrows and Tree Borrows model different aliasing rules. A result is evidence under the model used, not a complete language-level proof.
+- **Setup command**: `cargo miri setup` pre-builds the Miri sysroot so the first test run is not cold. Run it once per CI cache key.
+- Test unsafe abstractions through their safe APIs as well. A crate without its own unsafe blocks may exercise unsafe standard-library, dependency or generated code.
+
+## When It's Acceptable to Skip
+
+- Tests whose relevant operations are unsupported by the selected Miri version; isolate and test supported parts, and record what remains unchecked.
+- Generated code still merits testing when it participates in the safety boundary; auditing the generator is complementary.
+- Very large integration tests that make Miri impractical — run a targeted unit-test subset instead.
+
+Choose supported feature combinations; `--all-features` is appropriate only when those features work together. Record the nightly, flags, test subset and remaining gaps. See the [Miri documentation](https://github.com/rust-lang/miri) for current support and limitations.
+
+## See Also
+
+- [unsafe-maybeuninit](unsafe-maybeuninit.md) - use `MaybeUninit<T>` for uninitialized memory
+- [unsafe-safety-comment](unsafe-safety-comment.md) - document every unsafe block
+- [test-criterion-bench](test-criterion-bench.md) - use criterion for benchmarking (separate from Miri)
diff --git a/.agents/skills/rust-skills/rules/unsafe-no-mangle-unsafe.md b/.agents/skills/rust-skills/rules/unsafe-no-mangle-unsafe.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3820058
--- /dev/null
+++ b/.agents/skills/rust-skills/rules/unsafe-no-mangle-unsafe.md
@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# unsafe-no-mangle-unsafe
+
+> In Rust 2024, write `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]`, `#[unsafe(export_name = "...")]`, and `#[unsafe(link_section = "...")]` — not the bare attribute forms.
+
+## Why It Matters
+
+`#[no_mangle]`, `#[export_name]`, and `#[link_section]` were reclassified as unsafe in Rust 2024 because they can cause undefined behavior without any `unsafe` block at the call site. If two items in the same binary share the same exported symbol name, the linker silently picks one and discards the other — the "winning" symbol may have a completely different type, signature, or semantics. The result is type-level UB with no diagnostic from the compiler or linker. Requiring `#[unsafe(...)]` makes this footgun visible and auditable.
+
+## Bad
+
+```rust
+// Rust 2021 — bare attributes accepted, no warning about linker UB
+#[no_mangle]
+pub extern "C" fn init() {
+    // ...
+}
+
+#[export_name = "plugin_entry"]
+pub fn plugin_main() {
+    // ...
+}
+
+#[link_section = ".init_array"]
+static INIT: extern "C" fn() = init;
+```
+
+## Good
+
+```rust
+// Rust 2024 — unsafe(...) wrapper makes the risk explicit
+#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
+pub extern "C" fn init() {
+    // ...
+}
+
+#[unsafe(export_name = "plugin_entry")]
+pub fn plugin_main() {
+    // ...
+}
+
+#[unsafe(link_section = ".init_array")]
+static INIT: extern "C" fn() = init;
+```
+
+## Migration
+
+| Rust 2021 | Rust 2024 |
+|-----------|-----------|
+| `#[no_mangle]` | `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` |
+| `#[export_name = "sym"]` | `#[unsafe(export_name = "sym")]` |
+| `#[link_section = ".sec"]` | `#[unsafe(link_section = ".sec")]` |
+
+Run `cargo fix --edition` when migrating to the 2024 edition — it rewrites bare attribute forms to `#[unsafe(...)]` automatically. Review each one afterward: confirm that the exported symbol name is unique across the binary.
+
+## Key Points
+
+- The `unsafe(...)` wrapper does **not** require an `unsafe {}` block at the call site; it marks the *attribute itself* as load-bearing for safety. The annotation documents that the programmer accepted responsibility for symbol uniqueness and ABI correctness.
+- Symbol collisions are especially dangerous in plugin architectures, `cdylib` crates, embedded firmware with custom linker scripts, and any codebase that links multiple Rust crates into a single binary.
+- These attributes interact with `unsafe extern` blocks (see `unsafe-extern-block`): external symbols you import and symbols you export follow the same 2024-edition safety rules.
+- The bare forms (`#[no_mangle]` without `unsafe`) are a hard error in Rust 2024 edition code. They still compile in earlier editions but emit a deprecation warning with `--warn future-incompatible`.
+
+## See Also
+
+- [unsafe-extern-block](unsafe-extern-block.md) - wrap `extern` blocks in `unsafe extern` in Rust 2024
+- [type-repr-transparent](type-repr-transparent.md) - use `#[repr(transparent)]` for FFI newtypes
